<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mōri Terumoto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1553">1553</a>–<a href="/years/1625">1625</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Mori Terumoto</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mōri Terumoto (1553–1625) was a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> who ruled much of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a>, which he founded. One of the five regents appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, he was the nominal commander of the western army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600, though he stayed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a>. After the defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> reduced his lands to the provinces of Suō and Nagato, which became the Chōshū domain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto.jpg" alt="Mōri Terumoto" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mori_Terumoto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Terumoto was born Kotsumaru at Yoshida Koriyama Castle in Aki, the eldest son of Mōri Takamoto and grandson of the warlord Mōri Motonari. When his father died suddenly in 1563 he became heir, though Motonari kept real control of the clan's affairs. In 1564 he took the name Terumoto, the first part given by the shogun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiteru">Ashikaga Yoshiteru</a>. With his uncles Kikkawa Motoharu and Kobayakawa Takakage he put down an invasion of Suō by Ōuchi Teruhiro in 1569 and defeated Amago Katsuhisa at the Battle of Fubeyama in 1570. On Motonari's death in 1571 he inherited lands from Buzen to the borders of Harima and Bizen and a powerful navy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Motonari had advised against further expansion, but within three years his grandson was at war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>. The Mōri backed the rebel Ukita Naoie and supplied the Ishiyama Hongan-ji; in 1576, at the First Battle of Kizugawaguchi, Terumoto's fleet under Murakami Takeyoshi defeated Kuki Yoshitaka and broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>. In 1578 his uncles took Kōzuki Castle, where Amago Katsuhisa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamanaka-yukimori">Yamanaka Shikanosuke</a> were killed, but the same year Kuki drove off the Mōri fleet, and the Hongan-ji surrendered in 1580. By 1582 Hashiba Hideyoshi had flooded Takamatsu Castle in Bitchu, and Terumoto's relief force hesitated to attack.</p><p>After Nobunaga's death in the Honnoji Incident, Terumoto made peace and in 1583 became Hideyoshi's vassal, sending troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> and ships to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a>. In 1592 he led the 7th Division of 30,000 men in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, fighting partisans in Gyeongsang. He sat on the council of Five Elders and at his height held 1.2 million koku, enough to field more than 40,000 men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Reduced after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> to Nagato and Suō, worth 369,000 koku, Terumoto refused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a>'s call in 1614 and sent troops to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s side at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-osaka">Siege of Osaka</a>. When a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, Naito Motomori, was found to have fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>, suspicion fell on the clan until Motomori killed himself; Terumoto then forced Motomori's two sons to commit suicide. In 1616 he married his only daughter, Takehime, to Kikkawa Hiromasa to bind the two families. Resentment among his vassals led him to retire in favour of his son Mōri Hidenari, and he died at Yoshida on 2 June 1625. He was a noted patron of Hagi ware pottery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mōri Terumoto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Mori Terumoto's</b> son Hidenari ruled from Hagi castle, built from 1604, and the family's grievance against the Tokugawa was said to be kept alive for generations.</blockquote><a href="/state/choshu-domain">Choshu Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>His grandson <b>Terumoto</b> fought Oda Nobunaga, then made peace with Toyotomi Hideyoshi in 1582 and founded Hiroshima in 1589.</blockquote><a href="/state/mori-clan-domain">Mori clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Aki Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Akitakata, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.4,132.51&amp;year=1599" aria-label="Aki Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7919 918 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mōri Terumoto"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8369 1188h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8369 1188h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.4,132.51&amp;year=1599">What was here in 1599? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
